; `legacy-peer-deps=true` is load-bearing here, not stale. Strict peer resolution
; cannot be satisfied by this dependency set, and removing this line breaks `npm ci`.
; Both halves were verified by walking every peerDependencies entry in the lockfile
; and by running a strict-peer resolution outside the repo. node 24.13.0 / npm 11.19.0.
;
; 1. Two REQUIRED peers are unsatisfiable, not merely unsatisfied:
;      eslint-plugin-jsx-a11y@6.10.2  peer eslint ^3 || ... || ^9    (installed 10.13.0)
;      eslint-plugin-react@7.37.5     peer eslint ^3 || ... || ^9.7  (installed 10.13.0)
;    Both are already the newest versions npm publishes, so no upgrade widens those
;    ranges. `overrides` pins `eslint` to `^10.5.0` for them, currently resolving to
;    `eslint@10.13.0`, and that cannot be undone either: eslint-plugin-unicorn@78.0.0
;    declares peer `eslint@>=10.4`, so eslint 10 is mandatory and
;    downgrading to satisfy the two plugins is a dead end. npm
;    rejects the combination with ERESOLVE and will not nest a second eslint 9 for
;    them. The 46 other absent peers are all `peerDependenciesMeta.optional` and are
;    not blockers.
;
; 2. This lockfile was generated with the flag on, so peers npm would otherwise
;    install are missing from it -- 14 of them, including
;    `electron-builder-squirrel-windows@26.15.3`, a *required* peer of
;    `app-builder-lib@26.15.3`. `npm ci` therefore fails immediately with EUSAGE
;    ("Missing: electron-builder-squirrel-windows@26.15.3 from lock file", plus
;    electron-winstaller, @electron/windows-sign, postject, fs-extra, temp, ...).
;    No Squirrel.Windows installer is ever built -- `build.win.target` is `nsis`
;    only -- so that peer is unused at build time; it blocks resolution only, and
;    adding a scoped override for it would install a target this repo does not build.
;
; To drop this line, both halves must change in one commit: upgrade or patch the two
; eslint plugins so their peer ranges admit eslint 10, then regenerate the lockfile
; with strict peers (`rm package-lock.json && npm install`) so the peers land in it.
; Regenerating the lockfile alone is not enough -- it re-resolves nothing while this
; line is present, and `npm ci` re-checks the peers against package.json either way.
legacy-peer-deps=true

; Install scripts are deliberately NOT approved. There is intentionally no
; `allowScripts` section here: npm 11.19 prints an `install-scripts` warning on
; every install, and that warning is the policy working, not an oversight.
;
;   npm warn install-scripts 3 packages have install scripts not yet covered by allowScripts
;
; The three packages npm names, and why blocking each one costs nothing:
;
;   1. esbuild@0.28.2            postinstall: node install.js
;      This is the one that could have mattered. Its platform binary does NOT come
;      from the postinstall: it ships as an optionalDependency
;      (`@esbuild/win32-x64` and 23 sibling platform packages), and
;      `node_modules/.bin/esbuild --version` reports 0.28.2 on a clean install with
;      the script blocked. The postinstall is a fallback for platforms where the
;      optional package did not resolve, so skipping it is safe.
;
;   2. @tsparticles/engine@4.4.0 install: node ./scripts/install.js
;      Diagnostics only. It reads package.json and errors out if the incompatible
;      packages `particles.js` / `particlesjs` or the deprecated
;      `react-particles-js` are present. None are dependencies here, so the script
;      has nothing to report and contributes no build output.
;
;   3. es5-ext@0.10.64           postinstall: node -e "try{require('./_postinstall')}catch(e){}"
;      Also diagnostics only: `_postinstall.js` walks a list of IANA timezones and
;      prints a message when the machine resolves to one of them. It swallows its
;      own errors (`|| exit 0`) and writes nothing.
;
; Approving install scripts is a supply-chain change and needs its own review with
; its own justification; it is not granted incidentally here. To revisit:
; `npm install-scripts ls` to review, `npm install-scripts approve <pkg>` to allow.

; `typescript` is pinned to `^6.0.3` deliberately, not by neglect -- it is the one
; dependency `npm outdated` reports, and 7.0.2 exists. Both layouts below were
; installed, measured gate by gate, and reverted on 2026-10-17.
;
; 1. `typescript@7.0.2` is not a JavaScript library any more: 18 dependencies, all
;    `@typescript/typescript-<platform>-<arch>` prebuilt binaries, and `bin/tsc` as
;    its only entry point. There is no `ts.createProgram` for a tool to import, so
;    `npm install --save-dev typescript@^7.0.2` gives:
;      npm run typecheck       exit 0, 13s   the native compiler itself is fine
;      npm run analyze:types   exit 1       type-coverage needs ts.createProgram
;      npm run lint            exit 2       "typescript-eslint does not support
;                                            TS 7.0." from @typescript-eslint's
;                                            dist/index.js:50; nothing was linted
;    `typecheck` is the only gate that survives, which is exactly why it cannot be
;    the gate that decides this.
;
; 2. The side-by-side layout the TypeScript team documents for the 6.0 -> 7.0 move
;      "@typescript/native": "npm:typescript@^7.0.2",
;      "typescript":        "npm:@typescript/typescript6@^6.0.2"
;    does work: `tsc` 7.0.2 and `tsc6` 6.0.3 side by side, `require('typescript')
;    .createProgram` a function again, typecheck 13s -> 5s, type coverage 99.75%
;    unchanged, all five gates pass. It is still not adopted, for two reasons. It is
;    an explicitly temporary bridge: the 7.0 release post says 7.0 "does not ship
;    with an API" and 7.1 ships "a new (and different) API", so 7.1 obsoletes it.
;    And it made `lint` slower -- 51s -> 82s -- which was not investigated and is
;    not explained here. An unexplained regression in a blocking CI step is not
;    worth a compiler speedup on a codebase this size.
;
; 3. `@typescript-eslint/parser` declares `typescript: ">=4.8.4 <6.1.0"` on `latest`
;    (8.71.2) and on `canary` (8.71.3-alpha.10): no published channel admits TS 7.
;    `type-coverage` lists 7 in its peer range and still fails on 7.0.2, so that
;    range is aspirational, not tested.
;
; To revisit: when `@typescript-eslint/parser`'s `typescript` peer range widens past
; `<6.1.0`, Dependabot (`.github/dependabot.yml`, added in Task 5) opens the PR and
; that PR is the signal -- not this file. Full gate-by-gate measurements:
; docs/superpowers/plans/2026-10-17-dependency-freshness.md, Task 4 (a gitignored
; Superpowers plan file, so it may not travel with a clone).
